<SiteMeta Title="Accessibility statement" Path="/accessibility"
          Description="How accessible Gadfly Exams is today, what it does for students who need adjustments, its known limits, and how to report a barrier." />

<header class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Accessibility statement</h1>
        <p>How accessible Gadfly Exams is today, where it falls short, and how to tell us about a barrier.</p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="wrap prose">
    <p class="updated">Last updated 22 November 2026.</p>

    <h2 id="aim">Our aim</h2>
    <p>We want every student to be able to show what they understand, and every member of staff to be able to run and
        grade exams, whatever their abilities and whatever device they use. We are working towards the Web Content
        Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at level AA.</p>

    <h2 id="status">Where we are today</h2>
    <p>Gadfly Exams has not been tested with a screen reader or other assistive technology, and has not had a formal WCAG
        audit, so we do not claim that it conforms. We have been through the app a page at a time and fixed what we could
        check by reading the code and the rendered page: keyboard reach, visible focus, labels for form fields, colour
        contrast, motion, text resizing and layouts for tablets and phones. What that pass covered:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Keyboard and focus.</strong> A "Skip to content" link, present on every signed-in page as well as the
            public site, lets keyboard and screen reader users jump straight past the navigation. Every page shows a
            visible focus outline. The "Finish the exam now?" prompt moves focus to itself when it opens, so it isn't
            missed.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour contrast.</strong> Text, status colours and the border on every text box, dropdown and search
            field now meet WCAG AA contrast in both light and dark mode; this was fixed at the level of the shared colour
            variables the whole app draws from, not page by page, so it stays consistent as pages change.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduced motion.</strong> Every animation and transition we found — the examiner's fade when a student
            looks away, the "thinking" dots, the eye-tracking set-up dot, the public site's page animations — now also has
            a blanket "no motion" rule behind it, so anything added later without thinking about it is instant by default
            for a visitor whose system asks for reduced motion.</li>
        <li><strong>Layouts for tablets and phones.</strong> The exam room's picture-in-picture layout (the examiner, your
            own camera, the transcript and the answer area) already stacks into a single column below about 960 pixels
            wide, rather than needing a separate mobile design. Wide data tables across the staff and admin pages — the
            audit log, the grading queue, the assignments grid and others — now scroll sideways on a tablet-width screen
            instead of squeezing illegibly or breaking the page layout.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistent loading, empty and error states.</strong> Pages that show "Loading…" while they fetch data
            now say so to a screen reader too, consistently, across the admin, grading, review, assessments, analytics,
            sources and student pages, not just some of them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What we have <em>not</em> done in this pass, and know still needs work: we have not run the app with a screen
        reader, so we can't promise it reads well, only that labels, roles and live regions are in place where we could
        check for them by hand; we have not tested at 200% browser zoom on every page, only spot-checked the exam room and
        a handful of forms; and the admin and grading pages remain built for a desktop screen first — usable on a tablet
        (mostly by scrolling a wide table sideways), but not redesigned for one.</p>

    <h2 id="support">What the exam does to support students</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Captions.</strong> Every question the examiner speaks is also shown as text.</li>
        <li><strong>A live transcript.</strong> Students see what the recognizer heard and can correct words it got wrong before the answer is judged.</li>
        <li><strong>Reduced motion.</strong> With reduced motion turned on, or without 3D graphics, the examiner is a still portrait that still speaks; the eye-contact screen fade (when it's on) always comes with a line of text saying the same thing, so looking away is never signalled by colour or motion alone.</li>
        <li><strong>No penalty for how you speak.</strong> Judges are told to ignore filler words, false starts and transcription slips, and never to penalize accent or fluency. Pauses and speech rate are never part of the grade.</li>
        <li><strong>A fair clock.</strong> It starts when the first question is asked and stops while the camera, microphone or connection is restored.</li>
        <li><strong>Accommodations an instructor can grant.</strong> Extra time, and an eye-contact accommodation for students who find it hard to keep looking at the screen.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="limits">Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Exams are spoken, on camera.</strong> A student who cannot use a microphone or a camera cannot take a Gadfly exam as it is designed. Talk to your instructor about an alternative arrangement; the institution decides what to offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Speech recognition is less accurate for some accents and for stuttered speech.</strong> The transcript check and the instructions to judges reduce the effect, but do not remove it.</li>
        <li><strong>Staff pages are dense.</strong> Grading and review screens are built for large screens; on a tablet, wide tables scroll sideways rather than resizing, and on a phone they are harder to use still.</li>
        <li><strong>The 3D examiner</strong> needs a reasonably modern device; older devices get the still portrait.</li>
        <li><strong>No assistive-technology testing yet.</strong> What's above was checked by reading markup and CSS, not by using a screen reader or other assistive technology ourselves; some real barriers may remain that this kind of check can't find.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="report">Tell us about a barrier</h2>
    <p>If something in Gadfly stops you or makes it hard to use, tell us through the <a href="contact">contact form</a>
        (choose "Something else"), and say which page and what you were trying to do. If it affects an exam you need to take
        soon, tell your instructor as well, so they can make arrangements in time.</p>
</div>
